// BranchCleanup — the Target-tier CFG cleanup (EC11), on FULLY REGISTER-ALLOCATED PHYSICAL OPS.
// Shared by every backend that allocates registers (x64 and arm64); wasm consumes Std directly and
// never reaches this tier.
//
// Six transforms, all of them local to one function's block layout:
//
//   1. JUMP ELISION       — a block whose terminator is the ISA's unconditional branch to the block
//                           laid down immediately after it loses that branch. Control reaches the
//                           target by falling off the end.
//   2. CONDITIONAL INVERSION — a conditional branch to the NEXT block followed by an unconditional
//                           branch to the else target becomes the COMPLEMENTED condition to the else
//                           target plus a fall-through. Two instructions become one.
//   3. JUMP THREADING     — a block whose ONLY op is an unconditional branch is bypassed: every
//                           branch that named it is re-pointed at its target.
//   4. UNREACHABLE-BLOCK ELIMINATION — the blocks the threading (or the parser's `dead`
//                           continuations) left with no path from the entry are dropped.
//   5. COLD-BLOCK SINKING  — every cold block moves to the end of its function, so a guard's cold
//                           arm no longer sits between the guard and its hot continuation, and
//                           transform 2 then makes that continuation the fall-through.
//   6. LOOP ROTATION      — a loop's header chain moves to just after its hot latch, so the latch's
//                           back-edge branch becomes a fall-through (transform 1) and the exit test's
//                           conditional is the iteration's one taken branch: its own branch into the
//                           body, or the complement of its branch to an exit that now falls through
//                           (transform 2). One instruction fewer per iteration either way.
//
// ⭐⭐ WHY IT RUNS WHERE IT DOES — AFTER `applyAllocation`, AND NOT ONE PASS EARLIER. THIS IS THE
// WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT FOR TRANSFORM 3.
//
// SSA destruction places a phi edge's parallel copies ON THE EDGE, which for a critical edge means
// inside the `critsplit` block that was spliced onto it. BEFORE that runs, a block whose only op is
// a branch is not empty — it is an edge that is ABOUT TO RECEIVE COPIES, and threading it away would
// delete moves that had not been written yet. After `applyAllocation`, "the block's only op is a
// branch" is literally true and permanent: a `critsplit` that received edge copies holds `mov`s and
// is correctly left alone, and one that received none is genuinely empty and is correctly threaded
// away. That asymmetry is the reason for the placement, and it is not visible from the pass itself.
//
// The same placement answers the phi model: `applyAllocation` ends by calling `clearPhiModel`, so
// every block's `blockArgs` and `branchEdges` are already EMPTY here. There is no phi metadata for
// this pass to keep consistent — the ops are the only remaining statement of the CFG.
//
// ⚠ THE ONE LAYOUT CONSTRAINT: a block with no terminator op (a parser `dead` continuation) reaches
// the backend relying on physically falling into its neighbour, and the ops-derived CFG cannot see
// that edge. Transform 4 keeps such a neighbour alive on that edge (`dropUnreachableBlocks`),
// transform 5 moves the two as one unit (`sinkColdBlocks`) and transform 6 refuses a loop whose
// rotation would part them (`rotateLoops`); no other transform moves a block.
//
// ISA-neutrality is not an accident of the transforms: every question this pass asks is answered by
// a shared roster. `unconditionalBranchTargetOf` classifies both ISAs' terminators, `retargetControlOp`
// covers both ISAs' conditional branches and both jump tables, `Terminator.fallthrough` is a layout
// fact rather than an encoding, and the single per-ISA fact — a condition code's complement — is
// `invertX64Cond` / `invertArm64Cond` beside the enums they read (see `invertedCondBranchOnto`).

// The pass, over every function in the module. Called from `buildX64Backend` and `buildArm64Backend`
// between `allocateRegisters` and the frame pass: the frame pass then sees the SURVIVING blocks, so
// a callee-saved register or a `call` that only unreachable code used stops being preserved.
export function cleanUpBranches(module TargetModule, project Project)
	let probe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.branchCleanup)
	logDebug(LogCategory.codegen, message: function() gives "Branch cleanup: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage — the discipline `insertPrologueEpilogue` states.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		cleanUpFunctionBranches(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("cleanUpBranches: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'

	recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.branchCleanup, probe: probe)
end 'cleanUpBranches'

// ⭐ THE ORDER OF THE SIX IS LOAD-BEARING, in three steps.
//
// Threading and reachability both need a CFG, and the CFG is derived FROM THE OPS — a block's second
// successor is its conditional branch's target and its first is its terminator's. Transforms 1 and 2
// DELETE that terminator, which makes the else-edge unreadable from the ops (it becomes a layout fact
// instead). So every question that needs the CFG is asked first, the two reorders — the sinking, then
// the rotation — run while every else-edge is still an op (a block that has lost its terminator falls
// into its neighbour and could not move), and the elision is last — after which nothing in the
// compiler builds a CFG for this function again.
function cleanUpFunctionBranches(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	// The skeleton read off the ops, exactly as the allocator builds it — this pass derives no CFG of
	// its own. Locals are LAYOUT positions (`buildFuncTopology` numbers them in `func.blockRefs`
	// order), which is what makes "the physically next block" answerable as `local + 1`.
	let topo = buildFuncTopology(module, func: func)

	if topo.numBlocks == 0 'noBlocks'
		return
	end 'noBlocks'

	// Whether the function has a loop is asked of THIS topology, before the drop and the two reorders,
	// so the loopless function — most of them — never pays the fresh topology `rotateLoops` builds. It
	// can be asked this early because none of the three creates or destroys a loop among the reachable
	// blocks: threading re-points an edge through an empty block at that block's own target, the drop
	// removes only blocks with no path from the entry, and a reorder changes no edge. A loop the drop
	// removes whole costs one topology built for nothing.
	let hasALoop = NaturalLoops.find(topo.succ, nodeCount: topo.numBlocks as CsrIndex).headers.count() > 0

	let resolved = resolveJumpChains(module, topo: topo)
	threadResolvedEdges(module, topo: topo, resolved: resolved)
	dropUnreachableBlocks(module, func: func, topo: topo, resolved: resolved)
	sinkColdBlocks(module, func: func)

	if hasALoop 'loopToRotate'
		rotateLoops(module, func: func)
	end 'loopToRotate'

	elideFallthroughBranches(module, func: func)
end 'cleanUpFunctionBranches'

// The "no such block" answer for a column indexed by local: one past every valid local, so it can
// never collide with a real answer.
function noSuchLocal(topo FuncTopology) returns CfgIndex
	return topo.numBlocks
end 'noSuchLocal'

// A local where a block may have none to answer.
union OptionalLocal
	none
	at(index CfgIndex)
end 'OptionalLocal'

// The local a block's terminator branches to unconditionally — `unconditionalBranchTargetOf`, the
// one roster for that question, resolved through the topology.
function unconditionalBranchLocalOf(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, block IrBlock) returns OptionalLocal
	return match unconditionalBranchTargetOf(module, block: block) 'terminator'
		none gives OptionalLocal.none
		blockId(target) gives OptionalLocal.at(localOfBlockId(topo, blockId: target))
	end 'terminator'
end 'unconditionalBranchLocalOf'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 3 — jump threading
// ==========================================================================

// `resolved[L]` — the block a branch naming local `L` must be re-pointed at. `L` itself for every
// block that stays; the far end of a chain of empty branch-only blocks otherwise.
//
// Resolved with PATH COMPRESSION over an explicit path stack rather than a repeat-until-stable
// sweep: a chain of K forwarding blocks converges in K rounds of the naive form, which is quadratic
// in exactly the shape this pass exists to clean up (the inliners emit runs of them).
function resolveJumpChains(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology) returns CfgIndexArray
	let blockCount = topo.numBlocks

	// Classified in ONE pass over the blocks, so the chain walk below dereferences no block at all.
	var forwards = BoolArray.create()
	forwards.resize(blockCount)
	var forwardTargetOf = CfgIndexArray.create()
	forwardTargetOf.resize(blockCount)

	// ⭐ LOCAL 0 — THE ENTRY — IS NEVER FORWARDED THROUGH, whatever its ops. It is the block the frame
	// pass puts the prologue in, so a predecessor re-pointed PAST it would enter the function without a
	// frame. (The parser gives a loop its own header rather than branching back to the entry, so this
	// costs nothing today; it is the fact that must not have to be re-derived if that ever changes.)
	for local in 1 upto blockCount 'eachBlock'
		let target = emptyJumpTargetOf(module, topo: topo, local: local)

		if target == local 'staysPut'
			continue
		end 'staysPut'

		try forwards.set(local, value: true) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwards.set OOB at {local}")
		try forwardTargetOf.set(local, value: target) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwardTargetOf.set OOB at {local}")
	end 'eachBlock'

	let unresolved = noSuchLocal(topo)
	var resolved = CfgIndexArray.create()
	resolved.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: unresolved)
	var onPath = BoolArray.create()
	onPath.resize(blockCount)
	var path = CfgIndexArray.create()

	for start in 0 upto blockCount 'eachStart'
		if (try resolved.get(start) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {start}")) != unresolved 'alreadyAnswered'
			continue
		end 'alreadyAnswered'

		path.clear()
		var cur = start
		var answer = unresolved

		while answer == unresolved 'followChain'
			// The three facts about `cur`, read up front: a `try` may not sit bare in an `if` condition
			// (E3124), and reading them together keeps the chain below a plain three-way test.
			let forwardsHere = try forwards.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwards.get OOB at {cur}")
			let answerHere = try resolved.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {cur}")
			let onPathHere = try onPath.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.get OOB at {cur}")

			if not forwardsHere 'chainEnds'
				answer = cur
			end 'chainEnds' else if answerHere != unresolved 'answerKnown'
				answer = answerHere
			end 'answerKnown' else if onPathHere 'cycle'
				// A CYCLE of empty branch blocks is an infinite loop with no exit, so there is nothing to
				// forward it to. It resolves to the block the walk RE-ENTERED, which therefore stays —
				// threading the whole cycle away would delete the loop.
				answer = cur
			end 'cycle' else 'follow'
				try onPath.set(cur, value: true) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.set OOB at {cur}")
				path.push(cur)
				cur = try forwardTargetOf.get(cur) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: forwardTargetOf.get OOB at {cur}")
			end 'follow'
		end 'followChain'

		for local in path 'compress'
			try resolved.set(local, value: answer) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.set OOB at {local}")
			try onPath.set(local, value: false) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: onPath.set OOB at {local}")
		end 'compress'

		// `start` is on the path only when it forwards; a block that ends the chain was never pushed.
		if (try resolved.get(start) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.get OOB at {start}")) == unresolved 'startEndsTheChain'
			try resolved.set(start, value: answer) otherwise panic("resolveJumpChains: resolved.set OOB at {start}")
		end 'startEndsTheChain'
	end 'eachStart'

	return resolved
end 'resolveJumpChains'

// The local a block forwards control to when it is an EMPTY unconditional branch — no body ops at
// all, no conditional branch, just the terminator. Answers `local` itself for every other block, and
// for a branch to ITSELF (an empty infinite loop, which has nothing to forward to).
//
// "No body ops" is the whole test, and after `applyAllocation` it means what it says: a `critsplit`
// block that received this edge's parallel copies holds them as body ops and is refused here, while
// one that received none is genuinely empty. See this file's header.
function emptyJumpTargetOf(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, local CfgIndex) returns CfgIndex
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: local)

	if block.opRefs.count() > 0 or block.hasCondBranchOp() 'carriesWork'
		return local
	end 'carriesWork'

	return match unconditionalBranchLocalOf(module, topo: topo, block: block) 'terminator'
		none gives local
		at(target) gives target
	end 'terminator'
end 'emptyJumpTargetOf'

// Re-point every edge whose target resolved elsewhere, through the one edge-retargeting
// implementation in the compiler (`retargetControlOp`, SsaDestruction) — which covers the `jcc`, the
// `jmp`, the arm64 branches AND both multiway jump tables, so no edge kind can be silently missed.
//
// COLLECT FIRST, THEN WRITE: `retargetControlOp` writes `module.ops`, which no live borrow of a
// block out of `module.blocks` may overlap (E3070). It is also `splitCriticalEdgesInFunc`'s
// discipline for the second reason — the reads classify against ONE consistent CFG, and each
// redirect is independent of the others.
function threadResolvedEdges(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, resolved CfgIndexArray)
	var predRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
	var oldTargets = BlockIdArray.create()
	var newTargets = BlockIdArray.create()

	// ⚠ A SUCCESSOR MAY APPEAR TWICE IN ONE BLOCK'S LIST. `collectBlockSuccessorIds` pushes the
	// conditional branch's then-target and the terminator's else-target without deduping, so a two-way
	// branch whose two arms name one block lists it twice — and the SECOND redirect would find no edge
	// left to move and hit `retargetControlOp`'s no-match panic. Marked and cleared per block rather
	// than scanned: a dense jump table's successor list is long, and a membership scan over it would
	// make this quadratic in the arm count.
	var seen = BoolArray.create()
	seen.resize(topo.numBlocks)

	for local in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachPred'
		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(local) 'eachSucc'
			let succ = topo.succ.neighbour(local, k: k) as CfgIndex
			let alreadyListed = try seen.get(succ) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.get OOB at {succ}")

			if alreadyListed 'duplicateEdge'
				continue
			end 'duplicateEdge'

			try seen.set(succ, value: true) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.set OOB at {succ}")

			let target = try resolved.get(succ) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: resolved.get OOB at {succ}")

			if target == succ 'edgeUnchanged'
				continue
			end 'edgeUnchanged'

			predRefs.push(try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(local) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {local}"))
			oldTargets.push(blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: succ).id)
			newTargets.push(blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: target).id)
		end 'eachSucc'

		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(local) 'clearSeen'
			try seen.set(topo.succ.neighbour(local, k: k), value: false) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: seen.set OOB while clearing")
		end 'clearSeen'
	end 'eachPred'

	for k in 0 upto predRefs.count() 'eachRedirect'
		retargetControlOp(module, predRef: try predRefs.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: predRefs.get OOB at {k}"), oldTarget: try oldTargets.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: oldTargets.get OOB at {k}"), newTarget: try newTargets.get(k) otherwise panic("threadResolvedEdges: newTargets.get OOB at {k}"))
	end 'eachRedirect'
end 'threadResolvedEdges'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 4 — unreachable-block elimination
// ==========================================================================

// Drop every block with no path from the entry — the empty branch blocks the threading above just
// orphaned, plus whatever the parser's `dead` continuations already were. Survivors keep their
// relative order, so nothing that depends on layout moves.
//
// ⚠⚠ A BLOCK WITH NO TERMINATOR OP FALLS THROUGH PHYSICALLY, AND THE OPS-DERIVED CFG CANNOT SEE
// THAT EDGE. `Terminator.dead` / `Terminator.unset` name no op, so `collectBlockSuccessorIds` gives
// such a block NO successors — its real successor is whatever is laid down next, and dropping that
// block would silently re-point the live one at whatever survived further down. So the walk adds the
// physical edge, and it is added WITHOUT going through `resolved`: a fall-through is not a branch,
// threading never moved it, and the block it names must survive verbatim.
function dropUnreachableBlocks(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, topo FuncTopology, resolved CfgIndexArray)
	var reachable = BoolArray.create()
	reachable.resize(topo.numBlocks)
	var stack = CfgIndexArray.create()

	// The entry is local 0 and is reachable by definition — it is also the block that must stay first
	// (the prologue goes in it), which the ascending rebuild below preserves.
	try reachable.set(0, value: true) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.set OOB at the entry block")
	stack.push(0)

	while stack.count() > 0 'walk'
		let u = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: stack.pop on non-empty failed")

		// The edges as they stand AFTER the threading above: every branch target goes through `resolved`.
		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(u as CsrIndex) 'eachSucc'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, local: try resolved.get(topo.succ.neighbour(u as CsrIndex, k: k)) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: resolved.get OOB"))
		end 'eachSucc'

		if (not blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: u).hasTerminatorOp()) and u + 1 < topo.numBlocks 'fallsThroughPhysically'
			markReachable(reachable, stack: stack, local: u + 1)
		end 'fallsThroughPhysically'
	end 'walk'

	var kept = BlockRefArray.create()

	for local in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let survives = try reachable.get(local) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: reachable.get OOB at {local}")

		if survives 'keep'
			kept.push(try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(local) otherwise panic("dropUnreachableBlocks: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {local}"))
		end 'keep'
	end 'eachBlock'

	func.blockRefs = kept
end 'dropUnreachableBlocks'

// Mark a block reachable and queue it, once. Both columns are the caller's.
function markReachable(reachable BoolArray, stack CfgIndexArray, local CfgIndex)
	let seenBefore = try reachable.get(local) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.get OOB at {local}")

	if seenBefore 'alreadySeen'
		return
	end 'alreadySeen'

	try reachable.set(local, value: true) otherwise panic("markReachable: reachable.set OOB at {local}")
	stack.push(local)
end 'markReachable'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 5 — cold-block sinking
// ==========================================================================

// Move every cold block to the end of the function, keeping the relative order on each side. A
// guard's conditional branch names its cold arm and its terminator names the hot continuation, so
// once the arm is out of the way the continuation is the physically next block and transform 2
// trades the taken branch for a fall-through. Sound because it runs while every else-edge is still
// a terminator op: the order of blocks that carry one is a layout choice and nothing more.
//
// A block with no terminator op is the exception — it falls into whatever is laid down next — so it
// and its physical successor move as ONE UNIT, cold only when every block in it is. The entry's unit
// stays first: the prologue goes there.
//
// A sunk arm's branch spans the hot blocks that now sit between it and its guard. On arm64 that is
// the 19-bit `b.cond` / `cbz` field, and a function carrying more than ±1 MiB of code between the
// two is refused loudly by `arm64PatchBranch19` rather than mis-encoded.
function sinkColdBlocks(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	var hot = BlockRefArray.create()
	var cold = BlockRefArray.create()
	let blockCount = func.blockRefs.count()
	var unitStart = 0 as CfgIndex
	var unitIsCold = true

	for i in 0 upto blockCount 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(try func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("sinkColdBlocks: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}")) otherwise panic("sinkColdBlocks: blockRef at {i} not in module.blocks")
		unitIsCold = unitIsCold and block.heat == BlockHeat.cold

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() or i + 1 == blockCount 'unitEnds'
			if unitIsCold and unitStart > 0 'coldUnit'
				pushBlockRange(func, from: unitStart, through: i, into: cold)
			end 'coldUnit' else 'hotUnit'
				pushBlockRange(func, from: unitStart, through: i, into: hot)
			end 'hotUnit'

			unitStart = i + 1
			unitIsCold = true
		end 'unitEnds'
	end 'eachBlock'

	for blockRef in cold 'appendCold'
		hot.push(blockRef)
	end 'appendCold'

	func.blockRefs = hot
end 'sinkColdBlocks'

// Append the block refs at positions `from` through `through` of `func`'s current list to `into`.
function pushBlockRange(func IrFunction, from CfgIndex, through CfgIndex, into BlockRefArray)
	for i in from to through 'eachRef'
		into.push(try func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("pushBlockRange: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}"))
	end 'eachRef'
end 'pushBlockRange'

// ==========================================================================
// Transform 6 — loop rotation by layout
// ==========================================================================

// Lay every admitted natural loop's HEADER CHAIN down immediately after the loop's hot latch. The
// latch's back-edge branch then names the physically next block and transform 1 drops it; what the
// exit test at the chain's end does depends on its shape, and the loop is admitted only where the
// result is one instruction fewer per iteration:
//
//   • `jcc body; jmp exit` (the parser's `for` / `while cond` header): the conditional INTO THE BODY
//     is the taken back branch; the latch's `jmp`, and the not-taken conditional in front of it
//     before the move, are gone. Admitted whatever lies after the latch; if the exit does, transform
//     1 drops the `jmp exit` too.
//   • `jcc exit; jmp body` (a folded `while true` whose `break` test ends the header): pays only when
//     the exit block is the one physically after the latch, so that transform 2 turns the pair into
//     the complemented conditional into the body with the exit falling through. Anywhere else the
//     iteration would still run both branch instructions, plus the entry branch the move adds.
//
// Entering the loop costs one branch where the preheader fell into the header before the move, paid
// once per entry; the saving is paid once per iteration.
//
// Sound for the same reason as the sinking: it runs while every else-edge is still a terminator op,
// so the order of blocks that carry one is a layout choice and nothing more. The one implicit edge
// is a block with no terminator op falling into its physical successor, and a rotation moves exactly
// two blocks away from theirs — the block before the header, and the chain's last block, which is
// followed by whatever came after the latch. The loop is refused when either lacks a terminator op
// (the glue rule); the latch carries one by construction. The invariant every chain block keeps is
// that none lacking a terminator op is parted from its physical successor: an interior block is
// followed by the next chain block, except where it is itself another loop's latch and that loop's
// chain drains between the two — and a latch carries an unconditional terminator.
//
// The chain is the header plus the blocks it falls through with only cold arms beside it — a guarded
// load's fast path — up to the block with a hot exit edge. The loop is REFUSED when the walk reaches
// another loop's header (that loop's own rotation owns it), reaches the latch (a latch inside the
// chain has nothing to fall into), needs a block that is not the physically next one, or finds no
// exit test at all — a header branching into two hot in-loop arms, or a chain whose every exit is
// somewhere in the body behind it.
//
// Of a header's hot latches the PHYSICALLY LAST is chosen: the body's normal step block is laid out
// after every `continue`'s branch, so this is the latch the iteration reaches by falling through the
// body, and the `continue` branches keep reaching the header wherever it lands.
//
// Linear in the function: one topology, one loop finder, one shared membership record whose per-loop
// cost is the loop's own size, latches read from the finder's per-header buckets, and ONE rebuild of
// the block list. A function with many loops is the shape the compiler is measured on.
function rotateLoops(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	// The topology `cleanUpFunctionBranches` built is stale here: the drop rebuilt `func.blockRefs`
	// and the sinking reordered it. Locals are again layout positions.
	let topo = buildFuncTopology(module, func: func)
	let loops = NaturalLoops.find(topo.succ, nodeCount: topo.numBlocks as CsrIndex)
	var plan = RotationPlan.create(topo)
	var member = LoopMembership.create()

	for h in loops.headers 'eachLoop'
		planLoopRotation(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, member: member, plan: plan, header: h as CfgIndex)
	end 'eachLoop'

	if not plan.recordedAny 'nothingMoved'
		return
	end 'nothingMoved'

	func.blockRefs = plan.layout(topo)
end 'rotateLoops'

// Plan one loop's rotation: nothing for the entry (the prologue goes there, so it stays first) or for
// a loop with no hot unconditional latch.
function planLoopRotation(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, member LoopMembership, plan RotationPlan, header CfgIndex)
	if header == 0 'entryIsTheHeader'
		return
	end 'entryIsTheHeader'

	match lastHotLatchOf(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, header: header) 'latch'
		none then return
		at(latch) then planChainAfterLatch(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, member: member, plan: plan, header: header, latch: latch)
	end 'latch'
end 'planLoopRotation'

// With the latch known: collect the loop, walk the chain, and record it behind the latch if admitted.
function planChainAfterLatch(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, member LoopMembership, plan RotationPlan, header CfgIndex, latch CfgIndex)
	loops.collect(topo.pred, header: header as CsrIndex, into: member)

	match headerChainEnd(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, member: member, plan: plan, header: header, latch: latch) 'chain'
		none then return
		at(chainEnd) then plan.record(header, chainEnd: chainEnd, latch: latch)
	end 'chain'
end 'planChainAfterLatch'

// The physically last latch of `header`'s loop that is hot and closes the loop with the ISA's
// unconditional branch, read off the finder's bucket for that header.
function lastHotLatchOf(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, header CfgIndex) returns OptionalLocal
	var found = false
	var last = 0 as CfgIndex

	for k in 0 upto loops.latches.degree(header as CsrIndex) 'eachLatch'
		let tail = loops.latches.neighbour(header as CsrIndex, k: k) as CfgIndex

		if not isHotLatchOf(module, topo: topo, tail: tail, header: header) 'notAJumpLatch'
			continue
		end 'notAJumpLatch'

		if not found or tail > last 'laterLatch'
			last = tail
			found = true
		end 'laterLatch'
	end 'eachLatch'

	return OptionalLocal.at(last) if found else OptionalLocal.none
end 'lastHotLatchOf'

// Whether `tail` is a hot block whose terminator is the ISA's unconditional branch to `header`. A
// tail reaching the header through its conditional branch has nothing a fall-through could replace.
function isHotLatchOf(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, tail CfgIndex, header CfgIndex) returns bool
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: tail)

	if block.heat == BlockHeat.cold 'coldTail'
		return false
	end 'coldTail'

	return match unconditionalBranchLocalOf(module, topo: topo, block: block) 'terminator'
		none gives false
		at(target) gives target == header
	end 'terminator'
end 'isHotLatchOf'

// The last block of `header`'s chain — the first block along it with a hot exit edge, admitted by
// `admitChainEnd` — or `none` when the loop is refused (see `rotateLoops`).
function headerChainEnd(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, member LoopMembership, plan RotationPlan, header CfgIndex, latch CfgIndex) returns OptionalLocal
	var cur = header

	while cur < topo.numBlocks 'walk'
		// Chains are contiguous, start at a header and never step onto another header, so two cannot
		// overlap; a block already on one is a broken premise, not a loop to refuse.
		if plan.isClaimed(cur) 'onAnotherChain'
			panic("headerChainEnd: local {cur} on the chain from header {header} is already on another loop's chain")
		end 'onAnotherChain'

		if cur == latch 'latchReached'
			return OptionalLocal.none
		end 'latchReached'

		var hasHotExit = false
		var hotInLoopSucc = noSuchLocal(topo)
		var hotInLoopSuccCount = 0 as CfgIndex

		for k in 0 upto topo.succ.degree(cur as CsrIndex) 'eachSucc'
			let succ = topo.succ.neighbour(cur as CsrIndex, k: k) as CfgIndex

			// A cold arm beside the chain is not a layout constraint: it is already sunk, and control
			// that takes it comes back by branch.
			if blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: succ).heat == BlockHeat.cold 'coldArm'
				continue
			end 'coldArm'

			if not member.contains(succ as CsrIndex) 'hotExit'
				hasHotExit = true
			end 'hotExit' else if succ != hotInLoopSucc 'newInLoopSucc'
				// Both arms naming one block is one successor, not two.
				hotInLoopSucc = succ
				hotInLoopSuccCount = hotInLoopSuccCount + 1
			end 'newInLoopSucc'
		end 'eachSucc'

		if hasHotExit 'exitTest'
			return admitChainEnd(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, member: member, header: header, latch: latch, chainEnd: cur)
		end 'exitTest'

		if hotInLoopSuccCount != 1 or hotInLoopSucc != cur + 1 'noFallthroughContinuation'
			return OptionalLocal.none
		end 'noFallthroughContinuation'

		// A node is a header exactly when the finder bucketed a latch under it.
		if loops.latches.degree(hotInLoopSucc as CsrIndex) > 0 'anotherLoopsHeader'
			return OptionalLocal.none
		end 'anotherLoopsHeader'

		cur = hotInLoopSucc
	end 'walk'

	panic("headerChainEnd: the chain from header {header} walked past the last block {topo.numBlocks - 1}, which every step's successor check rules out")
end 'headerChainEnd'

// The exit test found: `chainEnd` ends the chain when the rotation PAYS — the two shapes `rotateLoops`
// states — and the glue rule holds. A block whose hot exit is not a conditional branch's (an
// unconditional exit, a jump table) has no test to make the back branch.
function admitChainEnd(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, member LoopMembership, header CfgIndex, latch CfgIndex, chainEnd CfgIndex) returns OptionalLocal
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: chainEnd)

	if not block.hasCondBranchOp() 'noExitTest'
		return OptionalLocal.none
	end 'noExitTest'

	let condOp = try module.ops.get(block.condBranchIndex()) otherwise panic("admitChainEnd: conditional branch op of local {chainEnd} not in module.ops")
	let condTarget = localOfBlockId(topo, blockId: condBranchTargetOf(condOp, block: block))
	let paysOff = member.contains(condTarget as CsrIndex) or exitFallsThroughAfterInversion(module, topo: topo, loops: loops, latch: latch, chainEnd: chainEnd, condTarget: condTarget)

	if not paysOff or not hasGlueAround(module, topo: topo, header: header, chainEnd: chainEnd) 'refused'
		return OptionalLocal.none
	end 'refused'

	return OptionalLocal.at(chainEnd)
end 'admitChainEnd'

// The second paying shape, `jcc exit; jmp body`: transform 2 fires after the move exactly when the
// exit is the block laid down right after the chain AND the conditional is the block's last body op —
// transform 2's own precondition, asked of the one helper that owns it (`invertibleCondBranchIn`).
//
// The exit is decided on pre-rotation locals, and `latch + 1` is exactly the block laid after the
// chain once `latch + 1` is not a header: a header's chain is skipped at its own position and laid
// elsewhere, so the exit would not be next after all. Nothing else that moves can sit at `latch + 1`:
// an interior chain block cannot (a latch's only hot in-loop successor along a chain walk is its own
// header, which precedes it), and in the stacked shape — a latch that is another chain's end — the
// outer loop's exit cannot equal its `latch + 1`, which is the inner body, a member. The equality
// already places `latch + 1` inside the function, so the bucket read is in range.
function exitFallsThroughAfterInversion(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, loops NaturalLoops, latch CfgIndex, chainEnd CfgIndex, condTarget CfgIndex) returns bool
	if condTarget != latch + 1 or loops.latches.degree(condTarget as CsrIndex) > 0 'exitNotNextAfterTheMove'
		return false
	end 'exitNotNextAfterTheMove'

	return match invertibleCondBranchIn(module, blockRef: try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(chainEnd) otherwise panic("exitFallsThroughAfterInversion: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {chainEnd}")) 'inversion'
		none gives false
		opIndex gives true
	end 'inversion'
end 'exitFallsThroughAfterInversion'

// The glue rule: the block before the header and the chain's last block both carry a terminator op,
// so neither relies on falling into the block the rotation takes away from it. `header` is never the
// entry, so `header - 1` is a block.
function hasGlueAround(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, header CfgIndex, chainEnd CfgIndex) returns bool
	return blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: header - 1).hasTerminatorOp() and blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: chainEnd).hasTerminatorOp()
end 'hasGlueAround'

// The rotations planned for one function: per latch, the contiguous run of locals laid down after
// it, and which locals those runs claim. A latch closes exactly one loop (its branch names one
// header), so the latch is the key.
type RotationPlan
	// `noSuchLocal` where a local has no chain behind it.
	var chainStartAfter as CfgIndexArray
	var chainEndAfter as CfgIndexArray
	var claimed as BoolArray
	var noChain as CfgIndex

	// Exported because a bare field is private to the TYPE (E3014); `rotateLoops` reads it.
	export var recordedAny = false

	static function create(topo FuncTopology) returns RotationPlan
		let noChain = noSuchLocal(topo)
		var chainStartAfter = CfgIndexArray.create()
		chainStartAfter.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: noChain)
		var chainEndAfter = CfgIndexArray.create()
		chainEndAfter.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: noChain)
		var claimed = BoolArray.create()
		claimed.resize(topo.numBlocks)
		return Self{chainStartAfter: chainStartAfter, chainEndAfter: chainEndAfter, claimed: claimed, noChain: noChain}
	end 'create'

	function isClaimed(local CfgIndex) returns bool
		return try self.claimed.get(local) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.isClaimed: claimed.get OOB at {local}")
	end 'isClaimed'

	// Record the run `header..chainEnd` behind `latch`. A latch closes exactly one loop, so a second
	// chain behind one is a broken premise; recorded anyway it would strand the first chain's claimed
	// blocks and surface only as `layout`'s count panic, far from the cause.
	function record(header CfgIndex, chainEnd CfgIndex, latch CfgIndex)
		let prior = try self.chainStartAfter.get(latch) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.record: chainStartAfter.get OOB at {latch}")

		if prior != self.noChain 'latchAlreadyCarriesAChain'
			panic("RotationPlan.record: latch {latch} already carries the chain from header {prior}; a second chain from header {header} cannot follow it")
		end 'latchAlreadyCarriesAChain'

		for b in header to chainEnd 'claimChain'
			try self.claimed.set(b, value: true) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.record: claimed.set OOB at {b}")
		end 'claimChain'

		try self.chainStartAfter.set(latch, value: header) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.record: chainStartAfter.set OOB at {latch}")
		try self.chainEndAfter.set(latch, value: chainEnd) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.record: chainEndAfter.set OOB at {latch}")
		self.recordedAny = true
	end 'record'

	// The rebuilt block list: every unclaimed local in layout order, each latch followed by its chain.
	// A chain's block can itself be the latch of another loop whose chain then follows IT — an inner
	// header whose exit edge is the outer back edge — so the chains pending behind the current block
	// form a stack of ranges rather than one range.
	function layout(topo FuncTopology) returns BlockRefArray
		var laid = BlockRefArray.create()
		var pendingNext = CfgIndexArray.create()
		var pendingEnd = CfgIndexArray.create()

		for local in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
			if self.isClaimed(local) 'laidAfterItsLatch'
				continue
			end 'laidAfterItsLatch'

			self.layBlockAndOpenItsChain(topo, local: local, laid: laid, pendingNext: pendingNext, pendingEnd: pendingEnd)

			while pendingNext.count() > 0 'drainChains'
				let top = pendingNext.count() - 1
				let next = try pendingNext.get(top) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layout: pendingNext.get OOB at {top}")
				let last = try pendingEnd.get(top) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layout: pendingEnd.get OOB at {top}")

				if next > last 'chainDone'
					_ = try pendingNext.pop() otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layout: pendingNext.pop on non-empty failed")
					_ = try pendingEnd.pop() otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layout: pendingEnd.pop on non-empty failed")
					continue
				end 'chainDone'

				try pendingNext.set(top, value: next + 1) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layout: pendingNext.set OOB at {top}")
				self.layBlockAndOpenItsChain(topo, local: next, laid: laid, pendingNext: pendingNext, pendingEnd: pendingEnd)
			end 'drainChains'
		end 'eachBlock'

		if laid.count() != topo.numBlocks 'blockLostOrDoubled'
			panic("RotationPlan.layout: laid {laid.count()} blocks of {topo.numBlocks} — a chain was laid twice or a claimed block had no latch to follow")
		end 'blockLostOrDoubled'

		return laid
	end 'layout'

	// Append `local`'s block, and queue the chain recorded behind it, if any, to be laid down next.
	function layBlockAndOpenItsChain(topo FuncTopology, local CfgIndex, laid BlockRefArray, pendingNext CfgIndexArray, pendingEnd CfgIndexArray)
		laid.push(try topo.blockRefByLocal.get(local) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layBlockAndOpenItsChain: blockRefByLocal.get OOB at {local}"))
		let chainStart = try self.chainStartAfter.get(local) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layBlockAndOpenItsChain: chainStartAfter.get OOB at {local}")

		if chainStart == self.noChain 'noChainHere'
			return
		end 'noChainHere'

		pendingNext.push(chainStart)
		pendingEnd.push(try self.chainEndAfter.get(local) otherwise panic("RotationPlan.layBlockAndOpenItsChain: chainEndAfter.get OOB at {local}"))
	end 'layBlockAndOpenItsChain'
end 'RotationPlan'

// ==========================================================================
// Transforms 1 and 2 — jump elision and conditional inversion
// ==========================================================================

// The layout walk, on the FINAL block list. Runs last, and nothing derives a CFG for this function
// afterwards — see `cleanUpFunctionBranches` for why that ordering is not optional.
function elideFallthroughBranches(module TargetModule, func IrFunction)
	// The LAST block has no physical successor, so it has nothing to fall through into. The count is
	// at least 1 here — `cleanUpFunctionBranches` returns for a function with no blocks, and the drop
	// above always keeps the entry — so the subtraction cannot leave the index type's range.
	for i in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() - 1 'eachBlock'
		let nextRef = try func.blockRefs.get(i + 1) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRefs.get OOB at {i + 1}")
		let nextId = (try module.blocks.get(nextRef) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRef {nextRef} not in module.blocks")).id
		elideBranchInto(module, blockRef: try func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("elideFallthroughBranches: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}"), nextId: nextId)
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'elideFallthroughBranches'

// One block's elision, against the id of the block that will be laid down immediately after it.
// A terminator that is not the ISA's unconditional branch — a `ret`, a multiway jump table, an
// op-less `dead` slot — carries nothing to elide.
function elideBranchInto(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, nextId BlockId)
	let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("elideBranchInto: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")

	match unconditionalBranchTargetOf(module, block: block) 'terminator'
		none then return
		blockId(elseTarget) then elideAgainstNext(module, blockRef: blockRef, elseTarget: elseTarget, nextId: nextId)
	end 'terminator'
end 'elideBranchInto'

// The two transforms, with the terminator's target in hand.
function elideAgainstNext(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, elseTarget BlockId, nextId BlockId)
	if elseTarget == nextId 'fallsIntoElse'
		// TRANSFORM 1. Whether or not the block also has a conditional branch: that branch names the
		// then-edge and is untouched, and the else-edge is now the fall-through it always was in the
		// emitted layout.
		elideTerminatorIn(module, blockRef: blockRef)
		return
	end 'fallsIntoElse'

	// TRANSFORM 2.
	match invertibleCondBranchIn(module, blockRef: blockRef) 'condSlot'
		none then return
		opIndex(condOpIndex) then invertBranchAndElide(module, blockRef: blockRef, condOpIndex: condOpIndex, nextId: nextId, elseTarget: elseTarget)
	end 'condSlot'
end 'elideAgainstNext'

// The op index of a block whose conditional half is a candidate for inversion, or `none`. Two
// STRUCTURAL things must hold, and the second is the one that is not obvious:
//
//   • the block has a conditional branch at all;
//   • ⚠ that branch is the block's LAST BODY OP. An op sitting BETWEEN it and the terminator runs
//     only when the conditional branch is NOT taken, so inverting the pair would move it onto the
//     other path — a silent wrong answer. `IrModule.appendOp` refuses to append past a recorded
//     conditional branch, but the live-range splitter mints its ops through its own append and
//     bypasses that guard (see `assertCondBranchIndicesComplete`), so this is CHECKED, never assumed.
//
// Whether it TARGETS the next block is decided where the op is read, so the branch is dereferenced
// exactly once — see `invertCondBranchOntoElse`.
function invertibleCondBranchIn(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef) returns CondBranch
	let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invertibleCondBranchIn: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
	let bodyCount = block.opRefs.count()

	if bodyCount == 0 or not block.hasCondBranchOp() 'noConditionalHalf'
		return CondBranch.none
	end 'noConditionalHalf'

	let condOpIndex = block.condBranchIndex()
	let lastBodyOp = try block.opRefs.get(bodyCount - 1) otherwise panic("invertibleCondBranchIn: opRefs.get OOB at {bodyCount - 1}")
	return CondBranch.opIndex(condOpIndex) if condOpIndex == lastBodyOp else CondBranch.none
end 'invertibleCondBranchIn'

// Invert the block's conditional branch onto the else target, and delete the terminator if it did.
function invertBranchAndElide(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, condOpIndex OpIndex, nextId BlockId, elseTarget BlockId)
	if invertCondBranchOntoElse(module, blockRef: blockRef, condOpIndex: condOpIndex, nextId: nextId, elseTarget: elseTarget) 'inverted'
		elideTerminatorIn(module, blockRef: blockRef)
	end 'inverted'
end 'invertBranchAndElide'

// THE ONE PLACE THE CONDITIONAL BRANCH IS REWRITTEN. False when it names anything but the physically
// next block, which is the case where there is no fall-through to trade the terminator for.
//
// The block is read for the target and let go before the write: `module.blocks.get` hands back a
// borrow of `module`, and `module.ops.set` writes that same storage (E3070).
function invertCondBranchOntoElse(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef, condOpIndex OpIndex, nextId BlockId, elseTarget BlockId) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(condOpIndex) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: conditional branch op {condOpIndex} not in module.ops")
	let thenTarget = condBranchTargetOf(op, block: try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks"))

	if thenTarget != nextId 'thenIsNotNext'
		return false
	end 'thenIsNotNext'

	try module.ops.set(condOpIndex, value: invertedCondBranchOnto(op, targetBlockId: elseTarget, condOpIndex: condOpIndex)) otherwise panic("invertCondBranchOntoElse: ops.set OOB at {condOpIndex}")
	return true
end 'invertCondBranchOntoElse'

// The conditional branch that is TAKEN exactly when `op` is NOT, aimed at `targetBlockId`.
//
// ⭐ EVERY PAIR HERE IS AN EXACT FLAG-LEVEL COMPLEMENT, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE INVERSION SAFE FOR
// THE FLOAT COMPARES. A condition code is a predicate over one flag state; `invertX64Cond` and
// `invertArm64Cond` each map a code to the code that is true on exactly the flag states it is false
// on, so for ANY flags — arm64's `fcmp` NZCV=0b0011 on an unordered pair included — exactly one of
// the pair is taken. Swapping which target is branched to and which is fallen into therefore cannot
// change which arm a NaN reaches. It is NOT a "logical opposite" of the source-level predicate:
// `mi`'s complement is `pl`, which reads TRUE on NaN, and that is exactly right, because `mi`
// (float `<`) reads FALSE on NaN. `arm64Cbz`/`arm64Cbnz` complement each other on a register's
// zeroness and never read the flags at all.
//
// ⚠ ON arm64 THE INVERSION MOVES THE ELSE-EDGE ONTO THE 19-BIT `b.cond` (±1 MiB) AND THE THEN-EDGE
// ONTO A FALL-THROUGH. Either edge could already exceed that reach before this pass — the then-edge
// rode the same field — so the exposure is swapped rather than widened, and `arm64PatchBranch19`
// still refuses an out-of-reach displacement loudly.
function invertedCondBranchOnto(op TargetOp, targetBlockId BlockId, condOpIndex OpIndex) returns TargetOp
	return match op 'cond'
		jcc(cond, _) gives TargetOp.jcc(invertX64Cond(cond), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64CondBranch(cond, _) gives TargetOp.arm64CondBranch(invertArm64Cond(cond), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64Cbz(reg, _) gives TargetOp.arm64Cbnz(reg, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64Cbnz(reg, _) gives TargetOp.arm64Cbz(reg, targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		default panic("invertedCondBranchOnto: op {condOpIndex} is recorded as a conditional branch but is not a `jcc`/`arm64CondBranch`/`arm64Cbz`/`arm64Cbnz`")
	end 'cond'
end 'invertedCondBranchOnto'

// Delete a block's terminator branch. `Terminator.fallthrough` is the slot's declared state for
// exactly this — *"terminator was deliberately elided at the target phase because the block falls
// straight through to the next block in layout order"* — and this pass is its only producer; both
// function-chunk emitters and the Target printer already emit nothing for a block that has no
// terminator OP. The branch itself is left where it lies in `module.ops`: nothing walks that array
// linearly, and rewriting every later op index to close the hole would cost far more than the word.
function elideTerminatorIn(module TargetModule, blockRef BlockRef)
	var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("elideTerminatorIn: blockRef {blockRef} not in module.blocks")
	block.terminator = Terminator.fallthrough
end 'elideTerminatorIn'
